MERIDIAN CLOUD SERVICES - OPS CONSOLE INTERNAL HANDBOOK (v3.2)
Fictional document for local LLM testing. All names, products and figures are invented.

1. COMPANY OVERVIEW
Meridian Cloud Services is a mid-sized hosting provider headquartered in the fictional town of Lindenbrück.
It operates three data centers: LBK-1 (primary), LBK-2 (secondary) and HAV-1 (disaster recovery only).
HAV-1 does NOT serve live customer traffic. It is activated only when both LBK-1 and LBK-2 are unavailable.

2. THE OPS CONSOLE
The Meridian Ops Console is the internal tool used by on-call engineers to view and manage incident tickets.
Current version: 4.7.1, released on 14 March 2026.
The Ops Console supports single sign-on through the internal identity service "Keystone".
The Ops Console does NOT support mobile browsers. Engineers must use a desktop browser.
Version 4.6 added CSV export of tickets. PDF export was planned for 4.8 but has been postponed indefinitely.

3. INCIDENT SEVERITY LEVELS
- SEV-1: full outage of a customer-facing service. Response time: 10 minutes. Escalate to the duty manager immediately.
- SEV-2: partial outage or severe degradation. Response time: 30 minutes.
- SEV-3: minor degradation, workaround available. Response time: 4 business hours.
- SEV-4: cosmetic issue or question. Response time: 2 business days.
A SEV-2 incident is automatically upgraded to SEV-1 if it remains unresolved for more than 2 hours.

4. ON-CALL ROTATION
The on-call rotation changes every Monday at 09:00 local time.
Each rotation has one primary and one secondary engineer.
If the primary does not acknowledge a page within 5 minutes, the secondary is paged automatically.
The Platform team (team code PLT) owns all SEV-1 incidents regardless of the affected service.
The Storage team (team code STO) owns backup and restore incidents.
The Network team (team code NET) owns DNS and load-balancer incidents.

5. MAINTENANCE WINDOWS
Planned maintenance is allowed only on Tuesdays and Thursdays between 22:00 and 02:00.
No maintenance is allowed during the last 3 business days of a calendar month (billing freeze).
If an emergency change is needed during the billing freeze, it requires written approval from the CTO, Dr. Ilona Varga.

6. BACKUP POLICY
Customer databases are backed up every 6 hours.
Backups are retained for 30 days in LBK-2 and for 90 days in HAV-1.
Restores older than 30 days take at least 24 hours, because they must be pulled from HAV-1.
Backups are encrypted, but encryption keys are NOT stored in the same data center as the backup.

7. KNOWN ISSUES
- KI-112: The Ops Console search may time out when filtering more than 10,000 tickets. Workaround: filter by date range first.
- KI-118: Ticket timestamps are shown in UTC, not local time. A fix was considered and rejected; this is now documented behavior.
- KI-121: If the migration to version 5.0 failed, engineers would need to roll back to 4.7.1 manually. Version 5.0 has not been released.

